# 📚 Arrays

> Arrays are one of the most fundamental data structures in computer science. They store multiple elements of the same data type in contiguous memory locations, enabling efficient access using an index.

---

# 📌 What is an Array?

An **Array** is a linear data structure that stores a fixed-size collection of elements of the **same data type** in contiguous memory locations.

Each element is identified by its **index**, starting from **0**.

### Example

```text
Index :  0   1   2   3   4

Value : 10  20  30  40  50
```

Accessing elements:

```
arr[0] = 10
arr[2] = 30
arr[4] = 50
```

---

# 🎯 Characteristics of Arrays

- Stores homogeneous (same type) elements.
- Fixed size after creation.
- Stored in contiguous memory.
- Supports direct/random access.
- Index starts from 0.
- Efficient for traversal.

---

# 🏗️ How Arrays are Stored in Memory

Arrays occupy **contiguous memory locations**.

```text
Address

1000 → 10
1004 → 20
1008 → 30
1012 → 40
1016 → 50
```

Because memory is contiguous, the address of any element can be calculated in constant time.

Formula:

```
Address = Base Address + (Index × Size of Element)
```

---

# 📊 Time Complexity

| Operation | Time Complexity |
|-----------|-----------------|
| Access | O(1) |
| Search (Unsorted) | O(n) |
| Search (Sorted - Binary Search) | O(log n) |
| Insert at End | O(1)* |
| Insert at Beginning | O(n) |
| Insert in Middle | O(n) |
| Delete at End | O(1) |
| Delete at Beginning | O(n) |
| Delete from Middle | O(n) |
| Traverse | O(n) |

\*O(1) when sufficient space is available.

---

# 💾 Space Complexity

| Case | Complexity |
|------|------------|
| Storage | O(n) |
| Auxiliary Space | O(1) |

---

# ✅ Advantages

- Constant-time random access.
- Easy to traverse.
- Cache-friendly because of contiguous memory.
- Simple implementation.
- Requires less memory overhead compared to linked structures.

---

# ❌ Disadvantages

- Fixed size.
- Insertion and deletion are costly.
- Memory wastage if allocated size is larger than required.
- Cannot efficiently grow or shrink.

---

# 🔄 Common Operations

## Traversal

Visit every element one by one.

```
10 → 20 → 30 → 40 → 50
```

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

## Access

Access any element using its index.

```
arr[3]
```

Complexity:

```
O(1)
```

---

## Searching

### Linear Search

Checks every element sequentially.

```
10 20 30 40 50
         ↑
```

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

### Binary Search

Works only on sorted arrays.

```
10 20 30 40 50 60 70
          ↑
```

Complexity:

```
O(log n)
```

---

## Insertion

### Beginning

```
Before

10 20 30 40

After

5 10 20 30 40
```

Requires shifting all elements.

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

### Middle

```
10 20 40 50

↓

10 20 30 40 50
```

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

### End

```
10 20 30

↓

10 20 30 40
```

Complexity:

```
O(1)
```

---

## Deletion

### Beginning

```
10 20 30 40

↓

20 30 40
```

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

### Middle

```
10 20 30 40 50

↓

10 20 40 50
```

Complexity:

```
O(n)
```

---

### End

```
10 20 30 40

↓

10 20 30
```

Complexity:

```
O(1)
```

